--TEST--
`Core\Command::run` converts a union-of-literals argument to the word itself, because the word *is* the value that type admits — `rule:tooling/commands-are-compiled`, `rule:types/single-value-types`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// § 6's "a matched value's type comes from the parameter", for the type that
// needs no conversion table to say what it accepts: a union of string literal
// types is already the closed set, and `nvs_types::routes::closed_set` reads
// it out for a command argument and for a route capture alike.
//
// The word and the value are the same here, which is what separates this from
// the enum sibling `command-run-converts-an-enum-argument-by-its-case-name`:
// there the word is a case name and the value is `rule:enums/no-class-machinery`'s backing
// integer, so the two have to be told apart. A member of a literal union has
// nothing behind it to become.
//
// Two members rather than one, because a one-member union is the same set
// written another way and would not catch a conversion that admitted whatever
// arrived: the sibling `command-run-refuses-a-union-argument-outside-its-set`
// asks the other half of that.
class Reports {
    #[Core\Command(name: "report", about: "Write one report")]
    public static function run("json"|"table" $format): uint {
        echo "format: ", $format, "\n";
        return 0;
    }
}

// The status is taken first, because the handler writes while `run` is still
// being evaluated.
uint $status = Core\Command::run();
echo "status: ", $status, "\n";
--ARGS--
report
table
--EXPECT--
format: table
status: 0
